# AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md hygiene

These files are always-on. Every line is a per-request tax. Bloated context
files can **lower** task success (ETH Zurich 2026; Anthropic; Augment).

## Shape

An **index**: commands, gotchas, and pointers to files the agent should read
*when that task happens*. Not a manual. Vercel: an 8KB docs index in
`AGENTS.md` beat unused skills.

## Litmus (every line)

Would removing this cause an agent to make a mistake? If not, cut it.

## Allowed

- Stack, commands, project-specific conventions, hard do-nots
- Filling template placeholders (stack, commands, trunk name)
- A pointer to a skill, rule, or doc (`session-handoff`, `docs/plans/`)
- "Read X when doing Y" — never "read X before every edit"

## Forbidden

- Pasting a skill or procedure body (workflow steps, review checklists)
- Style-guide dumps a linter already enforces
- Folder-structure or stack restatements the agent can see in the repo
- Situational how-to that only matters for one task
- Requiring a stack of docs before every typo fix

Nested `AGENTS.md` in a subdirectory wins for that area. Claude reads
`CLAUDE.md` (import `@AGENTS.md`); do not duplicate the same essay in both.

See `context-hygiene` when the chat itself is the problem, not the file.
